WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.530
Many men have worn the cowl since the very
first Batman film hit the big screen, and

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.290
every Batman actor has brought a different
point of view and a different feeling to the

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:09.290
role.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.519
The tones have spanned from angst-filled drama
to sly, suave crime capers, and it seems as

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:16.730
if there are hundreds of different ways to
play the Caped Crusader.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:20.580
Just before "Twilight" star Robert Pattinson
took on the Dark Knight for this year's "The

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.310
Batman," there was Ben Affleck, who was Bruce
Wayne and his gloomy alter ego in both "Batman

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.500
v. Superman: Dawn of Justice" and "Justice
League."

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.869
Affleck has always been outspoken about the
reason why he took the role on — and the

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:35.580
reason why he departed "The Batman" before
it went into production, which allowed for

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.390
Pattinson to board the project.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.560
The actor told The New York Times in 2020,

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.850
"I showed somebody 'The Batman' script.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.480
They said, 'I think the script is good.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.020
I also think you'll drink yourself to death
if you go through what you just went through

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.020
again."

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.150
But when he left the role, he ended up changing
the project in a completely unexpected way.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.020
How did Affleck's departure from "The Batman"
end up changing the face of the film forever?

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.011
Per a February 2022 New York Times article
about "The Batman" director Matt Reeves, the

00:01:01.011 --> 00:01:04.909
original version of the film was to feature
Ben Affleck's version of Bruce Wayne.

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:08.730
The film was slated to take a look at Batman
through a different lens — that of an older

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.280
Wayne, more mature, instead of a young trust-fund
heir covering up his vigilantism with an unmasked

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.729
life as a devil-may-care playboy.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:19.290
Affleck was also supposed to write and direct
the movie but eventually decided that he would

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.790
simply act in the film.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:25.240
Reeves himself eventually co-wrote the version
of "The Batman" which he would one day direct,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.450
along with co-writer Peter Craig of "Bad Boys
For Life."

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.050
Reeves then entered the picture after multiple
directors were suggested, and he said the

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.280
script he was presented with was "an action
set-piece adventure."

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.140
In the time it took for Reeves to complete
the final film in his "Planet of the Apes"

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.890
reboot trilogy, 2017's "War for the Planet
of the Apes," Affleck had left the project.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:44.909
Reeves said to The Times,

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:49.220
"I think Ben was just evaluating what he wanted
to do and it wasn't what I think he had fallen

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.360
in love with, in terms of playing that character
in the first place."

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.430
But his departure definitely affected the
future of "The Batman," as that meant Reeves

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.540
now had to forge ahead without his leading
man.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.710
He chose to do this in a way that would both
honor Batman's long past and push him to new

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:03.850
and different heights.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.610
And Reeves and Craig would have to be the
ones to take him there.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:10.579
In the wake of Ben Affleck's leaving the project,
Matt Reeves' goal was to present a Batman

00:02:10.579 --> 00:02:13.540
who would be changed completely by the events
of the film.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.620
Reeves told the New York Times,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.629
"I wanted the stakes of the story to challenge
him in a way that shook him to his core."

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:22.620
Instead of a movie about a "disillusioned"
Wayne forced to face that his vigilante ways

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.810
weren't getting the job done, the movie would
now center around, quote, "someone who hadn't

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.499
quite figured out why they were doing the
thing they were doing."

00:02:29.499 --> 00:02:31.790
Reeves continued to say that for his version
of Bruce Wayne,

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.569
"He's still stuck in those events that happened
when he was 10."

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:38.420
With this in mind, the project zeroed in on
a time period several years into his life

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:39.420
as Batman.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.980
Soon enough, Reeves had his new Batman in
Robert Pattinson — a choice that Affleck

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.809
ultimately approved of, calling Pattinson
a "good actor" during an interview — and

00:02:46.809 --> 00:02:49.569
the rest of the production's pieces slowly
fell into place.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:53.480
It isn't the film that fans would've seen
had Affleck still been Batman, but it definitely

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.739
seems to be a worthy entry.

